  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure continues to slowly shift east of New \nEngland this afternoon.  The mid and upper level ridge over the \nNortheast continues to flattens tonight.  The mid level flow becomes \nzonal, as a northern stream short-wave and a cold front \napproach from southeast Ontario and the eastern Great Lakes \nRegion for Thu-Thu night. Some mid and high clouds that moved \nin today will begin to thin tonight. A south/southwest flow of \nmilder air will occur ahead of the approaching front. The winds \nmay decouple over the Upper Hudson Valley/Lake George Region \nwith a partial clearing for some patchy fog to develop. \nOtherwise, some scattered lower stratus may form with low temps \nmilder than previous nights in the mid 30s to lower 40s. In the \nweak warm sector with the south/southwest flow tomorrow, temps \nmoderate above normal by 10 degrees or so with mid and upper 60s\nin the valleys (isolated 70F readings) and upper 50s to lower \n60s over the mtns. Dewpoints increased into the 40s to lower \n50s. The better forcing and low-level convergence with the cold \nfront does not arrive until Thu evening. The latest NBM \nguidance indicates the highest probabilities (30-60%) > 0.10\" of\nrainfall over the next 24 hrs ending 8 pm Fri will be for \nwestern Adirondacks. Lighter amounts are possible of a few \nhundredths into the western Mohawk Valley, western Capital \nRegion, northern Catskills, Helderbergs and southern Greens, \nwhere slight chance PoPs were expanded. Some sprinkles are \npossible further south and east. Cold advection occurs Fri \nmorning in the wake of the front with lows in the 40s to lower \n50s for most of the region.\n\nFriday features cooler and drier weather with breezy conditions. \nBetter mixing in the wake of the front will allow for west-northwest \nwinds 10-15 mph with some gusts 20-30 mph.  The strongest gusts will \nlikely be over the northern Catskills, within the Mohawk Valley\ninto the Greater Capital Region, northern Taconics and the \nBerkshires. We blended the NBM90 wind/wind gust with the \nprevious forecast. Temps fall back to seasonal levels, except \nslightly above normal from Albany south and east. The winds will\nbegin to subside Fri night, as another Canadian surface \nanticyclone builds in from Upper Midwest and Great Lakes Region \nover NY and PA. Lows will be on the chilly side in the 20s to \n30s. Many locations will be close to two weeks without \nmeasurable rainfall by Friday. Halloween looks dry and \nseasonably warm with the sfc high in control, as temps may \nmoderate slightly above normal to open November and continue \ninto early next week. There continues to be a lot of \nspread and uncertainty in the medium deterministic/ensembles/NBM\nguidance, when the next widespread chance of rainfall occurs \nnext week, which nows looks to be trending towards the mid week.",
